  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework relies on a specific period when Helsinki was physically isolated from the rest of Finland, which may not reflect typical urban mobility conditions during non-lockdown periods.",
      "what": "The authors adapted the BTH-SEIRS mathematical model to evaluate mobility reductions and pandemic dynamics within the Helsinki metropolitan area. The model incorporates mobile phone tracking data, railway usage figures, and hotel occupancy rates to simulate actual infection rates alongside economic activity indicators during a period when the region was isolated from the rest of Finland.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that a single spatial model can concurrently capture both epidemiological infection trajectories and real-time local economic activity driven by daily commuting patterns."
    }
  },
